WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:11.780 --> 00:00:17.160
What’s good yo? This week we cryin
havoc with Julius Caesar by William Shakespeare.

00:00:20.360 --> 00:00:24.780
In the 1st century BC, Julius Caesar and his army just cruised back to Rome

00:00:24.780 --> 00:00:30.740
after b*tch-slappin his rival Pompey. And now he and his entourage ragin through the streets to celebrate.

00:00:30.740 --> 00:00:36.100
It’s all poppin bottles and smokin kush til some Soothsayer calls our boy out and say,

00:00:36.100 --> 00:00:38.340
“You best Beware the Ides of March, yo!”

00:00:38.340 --> 00:00:42.060
But Caesar just shakes that hater off and rolls up another fatty.

00:00:42.060 --> 00:00:47.700
Later, some busta named Cassius conversatin with Brutus, Caesar’s main brutha from another motha.

00:00:47.700 --> 00:00:54.320
And Cassius be all like, “Caesar gonna become king and he ain’t even all dat. Dat fool’s got to go. You feel me?”

00:00:54.320 --> 00:01:00.440
Brutus dig, but he ain’t happy bout it. Caesar’s his boy, but he don’t want no tyrants messin with the Roman peeps.

00:01:00.440 --> 00:01:04.380
So he crew up with the cause, and the sh*t goes down March 15th.

00:01:04.380 --> 00:01:08.400
That day, Caesar bout to leave the
crib when his biddy Calpurnia say,

00:01:08.400 --> 00:01:12.780
"Baby don’t go. I seen some sh*t and just know something whack gonna happen.”

00:01:12.780 --> 00:01:14.440
He like, "oh sh*t, word?”

00:01:14.440 --> 00:01:20.040
But when one of Cassius’s thugs roll up, he convinces Caesar his b*tch be trippin, and then say,

00:01:20.040 --> 00:01:24.100
“Yo, if you whipped like dat, you can stay home instead of lettin’ us crown you King."

00:01:24.100 --> 00:01:27.800
Caesar be like, “Hold up brotha. Don’t be like dat. Let’s do dis sh*t."

00:01:27.800 --> 00:01:32.720
As soon as they reach the spot, all dem traitors whip out their blades and shank his ass.

00:01:32.720 --> 00:01:37.500
When Caesar sees his bro Brutus join
in, he say, “Aw sh** you too man?”

00:01:37.500 --> 00:01:39.600
Then BAM! Dat fool dead.

00:01:39.609 --> 00:01:46.260
At Caesar’s funeral, Brutus try sweet-talkin the Romans by tellin em that killin Caesar was a favor to Rome.

00:01:46.260 --> 00:01:50.860
And at first, erryone believe him. But Caesar’s boy Antony ain’t havin none of dat.

00:01:50.860 --> 00:01:55.300
He rocks the mic and makes all of Caesar’s killers look like real assholes.

00:01:55.300 --> 00:02:01.480
Next thing you know, the Roman people be burning down houses and kick Brutus and Cassius to the f**kin curb.

00:02:01.480 --> 00:02:07.080
So Antony, Octavius Caesar, and some rich playboy Lepidus start raisin a swole army

00:02:07.080 --> 00:02:09.360
to stomp dem backstabbin’ snitches.

00:02:09.360 --> 00:02:10.860
Snitches get stiches.

00:02:10.860 --> 00:02:15.720
Sh*t goes ham at the battle of Philippi, where brothas screamin “187” on the reg.

00:02:15.720 --> 00:02:22.320
Then Cassius think he see one of his homeboys, Titinius, get captured, gets all torn-up, and kills himself.

00:02:22.320 --> 00:02:29.220
But turns out ol' Titty just fine, but can’t handle Cassius bein dead, so he decide to take dat long dirt nap too.

00:02:29.220 --> 00:02:34.620
When Brutus’s army gets straight wrecked, he decide he gonna stack one more body — his own.

00:02:34.620 --> 00:02:41.160
Then Antony rolls up to Brutus’s body and pours one out for our boy, sayin he was the most legit Roman of them all,

00:02:41.160 --> 00:02:45.860
cuz unlike the rest of dem backstabbin rats, Brutus was actin for the good of the hood.

00:02:49.600 --> 00:02:54.320
Now if your b*tch-ass think you readin straight history up in here, you best check yo-self,

00:02:54.320 --> 00:02:57.480
cuz da bard wasn’t trippin about historical accuracy, B.

00:02:57.480 --> 00:03:00.940
Matter of fact, some of the details he pulled right out of
his assh*le.

00:03:00.940 --> 00:03:06.640
For example, in the play, ol' JC can’t hear sh*t out of one ear. And even though dat ain’t the way it was,

00:03:06.640 --> 00:03:12.420
Willy Shakes slangin dat detail to symbolize that although he can hear all the righteous things people say bout him,

00:03:12.420 --> 00:03:16.620
fool is completely deaf to all the warnings that his boys bout to cross him.

00:03:16.620 --> 00:03:21.120
"I rather tell thee what is to be feared / Than what I fear: for always I am Caesar.

00:03:21.120 --> 00:03:25.600
Come on my right hand, for this ear is deaf, / And tell me truly what thou think'st of him."

00:03:25.600 --> 00:03:29.940
And that’s part of what makes dis play a tragedy: Caesar’s head is so damn big

00:03:29.940 --> 00:03:33.360
that he thinkin he should rock the crown
even though it ain’t rightfully his.

00:03:33.360 --> 00:03:38.400
Caesar think he so hard dat ain’t nobody can touch
him, so he don’t listen to nobody —

00:03:38.400 --> 00:03:43.180
I mean, his wife, da soothsayer — and dat’s why his arrogant ass ends up in dirt.

00:03:43.180 --> 00:03:46.800
Cuz like Cicero say, people only
hear what they wanna hear.

00:03:46.800 --> 00:03:51.600
"Indeed, it is a strange-disposed time. / But men may construe things after their fashion.

00:03:51.600 --> 00:03:54.480
Clean from the purpose of the things themselves."

00:03:54.480 --> 00:03:56.820
And speakin of hearin, listen up, son.

00:03:56.820 --> 00:04:01.200
Even though the play is called "Julius Caesar," fool gets iced just half way through,

00:04:01.200 --> 00:04:06.400
so some scholas think that dis play is really about Brutus, and he's the real tragic hero up in dis hood.

00:04:06.400 --> 00:04:12.820
Ain’t no doubt that he wanted to put his boy Caesar six feet deep, but unlike Cassius, who just a jealous playa-hater,

00:04:12.820 --> 00:04:16.120
Brutus was tryin to create a free Rome with no tyrants.

00:04:16.120 --> 00:04:20.880
So as much as it hurt Brutus to backstab his realest homie, that wasn’t his tragic flaw.

00:04:20.880 --> 00:04:24.080
Naw, blood. It was rollin with the wrong crew.

00:04:24.080 --> 00:04:29.360
So roll with the right crew and hit
dat subscribe button! In vino veritas!

